  <section class="mb-10">
    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold tracking-tight text-stone-900 dark:text-stone-100 mb-3">Why Do Back Acupressure Points Matter?</h2>
    <p class="text-stone-600 dark:text-stone-300 leading-relaxed">The back contains some of the most-used acupressure points, partly because it carries the Bladder meridian—the longest meridian in the classical system—and its shu points, which are named for internal organs such as the lungs and kidneys. The region also includes GV14 at the base of the neck, a classic point for cold and immune support. This makes the back a natural target for lower back tension, fatigue, and respiratory complaints that are also covered in our condition pages and point-by-point location guides.</p>
    <p class="mt-3 text-stone-600 dark:text-stone-300 leading-relaxed">Traditionally, the Bladder meridian and its shu points are said to regulate the flow of qì to their paired organs, while GV14 connects the Governing vessel to the head and upper body. On a physical level, firm finger or tool pressure reaches the large muscles alongside the spine, where tension tends to gather during long sitting or heavy days. Releasing that tension gradually may make lower back discomfort feel easier, without promising any specific medical effect.</p>
  </section>

