  <section class="mb-10">
    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold tracking-tight text-stone-900 dark:text-stone-100 mb-3">How Do You Stimulate Back Points?</h2>
    <p class="text-stone-600 dark:text-stone-300 leading-relaxed">Some back points are easy to reach, but others, such as BL23 and BL40, are awkward on your own. Try the heel of your hand, a massage stick, a tennis ball pressed against a wall, or ask a partner to apply pressure for you. Allow one to two minutes per point at roughly 90 degrees to the skin, pressing both sides where the point is paired. For a sudden back injury, weakness, numbness, or pain that shoots down the leg, seek medical attention instead of pressing.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="mb-10">
    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold tracking-tight text-stone-900 dark:text-stone-100 mb-3">Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
    <details class="mb-3 rounded-xl border border-stone-200 bg-white p-4 dark:border-stone-800 dark:bg-stone-900">
      <summary class="cursor-pointer font-medium text-stone-900 dark:text-stone-100">Can I reach back points myself?</summary>
      <div class="mt-2 text-sm text-stone-500 dark:text-stone-400 leading-relaxed">Some back points are easy to reach, while BL23 and BL40 sit where most people need help. A massage stick lets you press points along the spine, and a tennis ball wedged against a wall applies steady pressure to the shoulder-blade area. A partner can often place points more accurately than you can on your own. Whichever method you choose, move slowly and keep the pressure level comfortable rather than painful.</div>
    </details>
    <details class="mb-3 rounded-xl border border-stone-200 bg-white p-4 dark:border-stone-800 dark:bg-stone-900">
      <summary class="cursor-pointer font-medium text-stone-900 dark:text-stone-100">Why does back pain respond to acupressure?</summary>
      <div class="mt-2 text-sm text-stone-500 dark:text-stone-400 leading-relaxed">Back discomfort often involves muscle tension alongside the spine, and acupressure applies steady, pointed pressure that can encourage those muscles to ease while breathing slows to follow. From a traditional view, the Bladder meridian carries the shu points linked to organs, so treating the back is classically connected to whole-body vitality. Responses vary: some find relief quickly, others prefer longer sessions. Serious back conditions always need a proper diagnosis from a clinician.</div>
    </details>
    <details class="rounded-xl border border-stone-200 bg-white p-4 dark:border-stone-800 dark:bg-stone-900">
      <summary class="cursor-pointer font-medium text-stone-900 dark:text-stone-100">How long should I press these points?</summary>
      <div class="mt-2 text-sm text-stone-500 dark:text-stone-400 leading-relaxed">For back points, one to two minutes per point at roughly 90 degrees to the skin is the standard range, a little longer than facial or acute points because the back muscles are thicker. Press both sides of paired points so stimulation stays even. Deepen slowly over 10 to 15 seconds and work toward a comfortable deqi response—deep, dull, achy, or full, never sharp—while breathing evenly. Short sessions can be repeated a few times daily.</div>
    </details>
  </section>

